From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: qwqgong-ui <noreply@qwqgong-ui.local>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:06:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] ping: carry the client TTL and return ICMPv4 errors

Backport of the ICMP rework in sing-tun v0.9.0-beta.2, which this fork's
v0.4.x base predates. Three defects it fixes, all of which make ICMP
diagnostics through the tunnel lie:

- WriteIP dropped the client's IP header and wrote only the ICMP message
  to a socket left at the kernel's default TTL, so `ping -t 1` and every
  traceroute hop reached the real destination. The TTL/hop limit now
  follows the packet, cached in lastTTL/lastHopLimit so a steady ping
  does not setsockopt per packet.
- The sockets were connected, and a connected ICMP socket only receives
  messages whose source is the peer, so the Time Exceeded replies transit
  routers send never arrived at all. Linux privileged and Windows now use
  unconnected sockets. Since Go's IPConn.ReadFrom strips the IPv4 header
  and only ReadMsgIP keeps it, the raw v4 read path switches to readMsg.
- loopRead dropped everything that was not an echo reply. Time Exceeded
  and Destination Unreachable are now matched against the outstanding
  requests through the datagram embedded in the error, with the wire
  identifier and the local source address restored in place, and relayed
  with the reporting router left as the source address.

An unconnected raw ICMP socket receives a clone of every ICMP packet the
host sees, so each flow would receive and discard every other flow's
traffic. filter_linux.go attaches a BPF program that keeps only echo
replies carrying this flow's wire identifier plus the two error types;
the identifier XOR also means the replies we inject into the tunnel never
match our own filter. If a Conn is reused for a second identifier the
filter is detached and left off rather than narrowing the socket wrongly.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
